reply.body:
I think it helps to show intention that there is some set of action flags, but it hides which flags exactly. Now there are 2 different places to look at. Also missing flag interprets as a false, but it might be a bug.

Personally, I find bare booleans in constructors/methods hard to read, not presence of extra flags. Need intellij support with inline hints (maybe github webui will them soon and it's no longer my problem :). I would prefer simple enums, also helps with static analysis. It does not take much space for enum, cant see any drawbacks.

```java
enum BypassCircuitBreakerOnReplica { Yes, No }

// it can be written nicely with enum pattern matching rather than verbose if/else with equality test

    void run(BypassCircuitBreakerOnReplica flag) {
        switch (flag) {
            case Yes -> System.out.println("yes");
            case No -> System.out.println("no");
        }
    }
```

I dont think all flags needs to be grouped by single enum. Other than they all booleans that change behaviour nothing in common. May be a single class that holds smaller enums to reduce files clutter.

